The training observations exhibit a clear strength-toughness Pareto surface whose extremes and knees are produced by a handful of distinct alloy/process families:

- very high toughness at modest strength arises from high-Cr/Ni austenitics given a simple high-temperature solution treatment (prototype 270 MPa / 350 J);
- a second high-toughness cluster appears in low-alloy Cr-Mo-V steels quenched and tempered near 750 °C (prototype 320 MPa / 280 J);
- an outstanding mid-front point is obtained from a 2.3 Cr–0.25 C–V steel given a high-temperature austenitize plus a 680 °C temper (710 MPa / 230 J);
- still higher strength at usable toughness is produced by 11 Cr–23 Ni–3 Ti maraging-type alloys after a two-step age (1000 MPa / 100 J);
- the high-strength extremity is realized by 11 Cr–W–V tool steels tempered at 400–450 °C (1330 MPa / 60 J).

Exact or near-exact compositional and thermal matches to each of these five prototypes exist in the candidate pool (c010, c068, c085, c044, c041).  Their predicted objective pairs are therefore mutually non-dominated and are spaced along the whole front; the hypervolume they jointly dominate is consequently larger than that of any set concentrated on a single family.

Final: c010, c041, c044, c068, c085
